class DeviceStatus(BaseModel):
    """
    Aggregated state of the robot, for a client that holds no session on it: it answers whether the robot can be used right now, and which robot it is, without the four authenticated reads of `GET /api/device/id`, `GET /api/robot/config`, `GET /api/firmware` and `GET /api/programs-executor/status`.
    """

    status: Status
    """
    Exactly one state, resolved in this order — a robot both held by a workstation
    and in emergency stop reports the stop, which is what an operator has to see
    first.

    | order | value | meaning |
    |---|---|---|
    | 1 | `unknown` | no safety state to read: the firmware container is not running, or none has been received yet |
    | 2 | `emergency_stop` | an emergency stop input is active |
    | 3 | `protective_stop` | a recoverable stop is active |
    | 4 | `running` | a program execution or a move command is under way, a paused one included |
    | 5 | `in_use` | a workstation holds the control session and the robot is not moving, see `client_id` |
    | 6 | `available` | none of the above, the robot is free. A disarmed robot is available: that is its state at rest |

    `unknown` comes first on purpose: with no safety state, `available` would be a
    guess on whether a stop is active.

    """
    client_id: str | None = Field(
        None, max_length=64, pattern="^studio-[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$"
    )
    """
    MQTT client id of the workstation holding the control session, absent while the
    robot is free. Reported whatever `status` says: a robot held by another
    workstation stays held while it is in emergency stop, and that is what forbids
    opening it. A workstation that loses its connection keeps the robot for 15
    seconds, the time to reconnect; one that says it is leaving gives it up at once.

    Reported to the byte: the value is the one the workstation connected under, with
    no normalization and no truncation. Its shape and the character set it is taken
    from live with the `local` server of the AsyncAPI document.

    This is the value a workstation announces in the `Client-Id` header of a driving
    command.

    """
    manual_mode: bool | None = None
    """
    Only manual control modes are authorized; when false, only automatic modes are. Same source as `safety.manual_mode` on `GET /api/firmware`, and absent for the same reason `status` reads `unknown`: nothing has reported it.
    """
    armed: bool | None = None
    """
    The robot is armed and accepts commands. Same source as `safety.armed` on `GET /api/firmware`, and absent for the same reason `manual_mode` is: nothing has reported a safety state. Not readable from `status`, where an armed robot and a disarmed one both answer `available`.
    """
    device_name: str | None = Field(None, examples=["App - QA"])
    """
    Name the device manager gives the robot, a fixed identifier rather than a label an operator picks, and absent when it has none to give. Same value as `device_name` on `GET /api/device/id`, read once when the robot's API starts.
    """
    model: str | None = Field(None, examples=["nr2m"])
    """
    Robot model, the value the robot is configured with, absent when it is not configured. Same value as `model` on `GET /api/robot/config`, and unlike that route it answers with the firmware stopped. Reported raw, a display label is the client's own business.
    """
    hostname: str | None = Field(None, examples=["6ff4823.local"])
    """
    Fully qualified name the robot answers to on the local network, the one it publishes over mDNS. Absent when no name has been resolved.
    """
    ip: str | None = Field(None, examples=["192.168.1.19"])
    """
    Address the client dialled to reach the robot, read back from the authority of the request, so a client holds the address it reached the robot on rather than one of the robot's other interfaces. A client that dialled the robot by name gets nothing: the robot serves from a container and cannot name the address behind that name. Reported in its plain form, an IPv6 address stripped of the brackets the authority carried, so a client building a URL from it puts them back.
    """
    interfaces: list[DeviceInterface] | None = None
    """
    Every interface the robot is connected on, with the address it holds there. Where `ip` answers what a client dialled, this answers where the robot itself is reachable, so a client that dialled it by name still has an address to show and knows which interface carries it. Absent when no interface is connected.
    """
    execution: DeviceExecution | None = None


